# Private-term blocklist — the leak gate's VOCABULARY layer.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES, AND WHY IT IS A TEMPLATE RATHER THAN A REAL LIST.
# Copy it into your own individual practice set (the private repository
# named in your user-level config), fill it in there, and point the
# environment variable at it:
#
#     cp templates/leak-blocklist.txt.template \
#        <your individual set>/leak-blocklist.txt
#     export PRECEDENT_LEAK_BLOCKLIST=<your individual set>/leak-blocklist.txt
#     git config precedent.requireVocabulary true
#
# It must NOT be filled in here. A list of the words you are keeping private,
# committed to a public repository, publishes the words it exists to guard —
# and Precedent is a branch of a public repo, so a push cannot be taken back.
# The leak gate refuses a blocklist path inside this repository, by name.
# This is the same arrangement `scrub-gate` already uses: the blocklist lives
# in the private repo and scans the public tree from there.
#
# The `git config` line above matters as much as the export. Without it the
# vocabulary layer fails OPEN: a shell that starts without the variable set
# scans only the structural rules and exits 0, so the push goes through.
#
# FORMAT. One Python regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively
# against every file's text and every commit message in whatever the gate is
# scanning. Blank lines and lines beginning with `#` are ignored. An invalid
# expression fails the gate rather than being skipped.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN IT. Words that are private *because of who they name*,
# which no structural rule can recognise: client and employer names, project
# code words, internal system and service names, unreleased product names,
# people's names where the association is the sensitive part, internal
# hostnames and ticket prefixes. The structural layer already covers shapes —
# email addresses, home directories, individual- and team-level paths — so
# there is no need to restate those here.
#
# WRITING THE PATTERNS. Prefer `\b…\b` so a term does not match inside an
# unrelated word. Cover the spellings the term actually appears in: hyphenated
# and spaced, singular and possessive, the abbreviation as well as the full
# name. A term that only ever appears one way in your notes today will appear
# another way the first time it matters.
#
# The entries below are placeholders, and are here to show the shape. Replace
# them with your own terms.
#
# They are deliberately spelled as unmistakable non-words rather than as
# plausible examples. A first draft of this file shipped `\bacme[- ]?corp\b`
# as its example, and the very first run of the gate failed — on "Acme Corp"
# in this repository's own style-guide template, which is placeholder text and
# not a leak. A gate that fails on innocent text the day you install it is a
# gate you uninstall, so the shipped examples cannot be words anything else
# might legitimately contain. If one of these ever fires, it means this file
# was copied and not edited.

\bexample-client-name-goes-here\b
\bexample-code-word-goes-here\b
\bEXAMPLE-TICKET-\d+\b
